### Feature Engineering
# pd.to_datetime(df['DateID'], format='%Y%m%d') converts values formatted like YYYYMMDD into actual datetime values and stores them in a new WatchDate column. This makes it possible to extract calendar features such as day and month. df.head(3) previews the first three rows to check the conversion
df['WatchDate'] = pd.to_datetime(df['DateID'], format='%Y%m%d')

df.head(3)
%md
#### Feature engineering: extracting calendar features
The `.dt` accessor extracts parts of the `WatchDate` datetime column:
- `day_name()` creates the weekday name.
- `month_name()` creates the month name.
- `.dt.year` creates the year.
- `.dt.day` creates the day number within the month.

These derived columns make it easier to compare viewing patterns by day, month, or year. `head(3)` checks the newly created features on a small sample.

# This line of code is to extract the MonthName from the WatchDate column
df_cleaned ["MonthName"]=df_cleaned['WatchDate'].dt.month_name()

# This line of code is to extract the year from the WatchDate column
df_cleaned ['year']=df_cleaned['WatchDate'].dt.year

# This line of code is to extract the day of month from the WatchDate column
df_cleaned ['day']=df_cleaned['WatchDate'].dt.day

#displayig the first 3 rows in order to check the changes made to the dataframe
df_cleaned.head(3)
%md
#### Feature engineering: classifying weekdays and weekends
The code first assigns every row the label `Weekday`, then uses `.loc[...]` to change Saturday and Sunday rows to `Weekend`. This creates a categorical feature for comparing viewing behaviour across these two day groups.



df_cleaned['DayClassification'] = 'Weekday' # Set a default first
df_cleaned.loc[df_cleaned['DayName'] =='Sunday', 'DayClassification'] = 'Weekend' 
df_cleaned.loc[df_cleaned['DayName'] =='Saturday', 'DayClassification'] = 'Weekend'
display(df_cleaned)

